Kitchen speed is often a storage problem wearing a chef coat. At St. Francis, new employees, dish workers, and cooks burned minutes on hunts for pans, lids, chemicals, and backup tools because **nothing lived where the work happened**.

I reorganized on behalf of management using functional layout principles: group by task frequency, keep heavy-use paths short, and separate “daily reach” from “monthly crawl” storage @cite[keys="wp-st-francis-story,ois-organizing"].

## Chef office and working closets

The chef office had become a paperwork and junk gravity well. Cleaning and re-strategizing that space was part of making management decisions visible to the floor @cite[key="ois-organizing"].

Kitchen closets near the line got the same treatment: labels, shelf logic, and Metro rack adjustments where casters and pan sizing notes in the Drive corpus mattered @cite[key="sop-metro-notes"].

## Purchased equipment

When racks and cleaning tools were the bottleneck, I helped justify purchases — new storage hardware and cleaning equipment that paid back in labor @cite[key="ois-organizing"]. That is boring procurement work, but it is how “we should be cleaner” becomes physically possible.

## Long-term storage

Dry storage floors and back closets held years of “we might need this” without a map. The deep-clean post covers the fork-in-the-floor extreme; this post covers **where** items landed after the purge and scrub @cite[key="st-francis-deep-clean"].

## Documented responsibilities

Prep team vs nutrition team boundaries show up in the indexed Kitchen Team / Nutrition Team responsibility notes @cite[key="sop-team-roles"]. Reorg only sticks when job descriptions match shelf labels.

